    sum: "Red-cedar-bark regalia for the Hamat'sa — the wild one tamed by dance, ringed in shredded bark and transformation.",
    desc: "The Hamat'sa ('cannibal dance') dramatizes a youth seized by Man-Eater-at-North-End-of-World and slowly tamed through ceremonial dances at a potlatch. His regalia is deliberately feral: shredded red cedar-bark head ring and waist loops that explode with motion, hemlock-branch props, bird whistles answered from the rafters, and carved regalia — raven rattles, transformation masks snapping from one being to another on concealed levers. Field photographs of 1914-1930s winter dances document the iconic look. Canada's potlatch ban (1885-1951) jailed dancers and confiscated regalia; the repatriated treasures anchor the U'mista Cultural Centre in Alert Bay, where the dance is performed again. The look: crimson bark storm around a black-painted body, feathers and masks in ferocious orbit.",
