    sum: "Three rooms of 8th-century fresco — royal presentation, war and torture, and a triumph dance in full color.",
    desc: "Bonampak's Structure 1 preserves the most complete Maya mural cycle known: three vaulted rooms painted around AD 790 under lord Chan Muwan, chronicling his court — the presentation of an heir among elaborately robed nobles; the storming of a village, captives bleeding before the throne; and a final dance in feathered regalia with musicians and masked performers. Painters on lime stucco handled wet and dry pigment in mineral reds, blues (Maya blue), yellows and blacks with astonishing life — eyebrows, teeth, textiles, glyphs naming every figure. Lost to jungle and found in 1946 (guided by Lacandon Maya), the murals exploded the 'peaceful Maya' myth, showing courtly politics and violence in HD. INAH's reproductions and digital scans keep the fragile originals sheltered. The look: deep-walled fresco theater, oxblood and teal, glyph-tagged drama.",
