    sum: "Four surviving bark-paper screenfolds — Venus tables, rain almanacs and gods painted in red-black calligraphy.",
    desc: "Maya scribes (aj tz'ib) folded lime-coated bark paper (huun) into accordion screenfolds, painting gods, rituals and astronomical tables with reed brushes in a red-black outline style dense with glyphs. The Dresden Codex's Venus tables and eclipse almanacs track the morning star to uncanny accuracy; the Madrid Book is a household-ritual compendium; Paris holds the katun-prophecy pages; the Grolier — re-authenticated as the Maya Codex of Mexico in 2018 — is the oldest known book in the Americas. Bishop Diego de Landa's 1562 burning of 'books of the devil' left only these four, making them the last voices of a vast scribal culture; their figures and color scheme still define Maya visual identity. The look: red-cased gods in black ink, columned almanacs, numerical dots and bars.",
