    sum: "Deer-hide screenfolds painting genealogies and conquests — Eight Deer Jaguar Claw's world in red-line graphics.",
    desc: "Mixtec painted histories record eight centuries of Ñuu Dzaui royalty: genealogies, marriages and wars of rulers like Eight Deer Jaguar Claw (Codex Zouche-Nuttall), creation at Apoala (Vindobonensis), and tribute lands (Colombino). Deerskin screenfolds, gessoed to a hard white ground, carry flat figures drawn in fine red outlines with black and color fills; people are named by day-sign and numeral glyphs so each noble is a walking date. The crisp 'Mixteca-Puebla' style became the international idiom of late Postclassic Mesoamerica, shaping Aztec imperial art, and its linearity fascinates modern designers. Held at the British Museum, Vienna and Mexico's national library, the books are read today in Mixtec communities' revived literacy projects. The look: banded place-glyphs, profile nobles, red-line precision on warm hide.",
