    sum: "Faces of gods in thousands of turquoise tesserae — serpent bodies, skull eyes, resin-set precision.",
    desc: "Mexica artists set thousands of hand-cut turquoise tesserae over carved cedar faces and figures, following every curve with resin adhesive so a god's face ripples with mineral blue-green light. Masterpieces carried to Europe in the 1520s and now icon-class: the Double-headed Serpent, the turquoise Mask of Xiuhtecuhtli (god of fire and year) and Tezcatlipoca's skull-face mask, mostly in the British Museum. Tesserae came from trade routes reaching far north of the empire; shells, pyrite and mother-of-pearl flash accents; red Spondylus-shell eyes bite from some masks. Fewer than a dozen mosaics survive — sacrificial and diplomatic regalia burned or melted in conquest — making each an unmatched window into Mexica taste. The look: dense blue-green shingle, polished stone glow, eerie symmetry.",
