    sum: "Mesoamerica's only true copper state — smelted axes and bells, now hammered vases of Santa Clara del Cobre.",
    desc: "The Purépecha (Tarascan) Empire was Mesoamerica's metallurgical exception: the only state that smelted and cast copper on an industrial scale, producing cold-hammered axes, needles, awls and cast tinkler bells from ore roasted in open furnaces. Copper tools and bells were tribute, war material and ritual sound; the Relación de Michoacán documents the craft's status. After conquest the skill survived — Santa Clara del Cobre's smiths retooled for colonial trade and today hammer world-famous vases and single-piece cauldrons, some over a meter wide, from reclaimed copper, a tradition crowned each year at the Feria del Cobre, where master smiths compete to raise single-piece vases. The look: fire-crimson copper settling into deep warm patina, hammer-bloomed surfaces.",
