    sum: "Four flyers descend spinning from a 30-meter pole while a caporal plays flute and drum above — 52 revolutions of prayer.",
    desc: "Five Totonac men climb a lashed 30-meter pole: atop, the caporal stands playing reed flute and small drum without tether, while the four voladores bind ropes around the pole and launch into the void, spinning down in widening circles — thirteen turns each, four flyers, fifty-two revolutions for the calendar round. The rite petitions for rain and fertility, remembered in Totonac myth as an answer to drought, and was historically performed from trees. UNESCO inscribed the Ritual Ceremony of the Voladores in 2009; beside authentic Papantla performances, tourist versions (Xochimilco, airports) raise heritage-and-commodification debates. Painted flyers in red-and-white suits and ribboned headdresses climb in solemn order. The look: red-and-white figures spiraling against sky, drum-flute circling overhead.",
