    sum: "Hummingbirds, monkeys and spiders scraped into desert pavement — hundreds of figures readable only from the air.",
    desc: "Across the Peruvian pampa, Nazca makers removed dark iron-oxide pebbles to expose pale ground beneath, drawing hundreds of geoglyphs: the hummingbird's long beak, spiral-tailed monkey, spider, condor, whale and tree, plus thousands of straight lines and trapezoids marching to the horizon. Surveying was done with stakes and cords by coordinated crews; the dry, windless desert preserved them for two millennia. Purposes argued — water rituals tied to aquifer-fed springs, pilgrimage processions along the lines, offering images to mountain and sky deities — remain debated, with drone surveys still finding new figures (the 2019-2020 'cat' and AI-found hillside glyphs). Maria Reiche's fifty-year guardianship and UNESCO listing (1994) fixed their fame; flights over the pampa are now the classic viewing rite. The look: single-stroke animal calligraphy on rust-red earth.",
