    sum: "Stirrup-spout bottles modeling individual faces — age, scar and sneer captured in fired clay.",
    desc: "Moche potters made the pre-Columbian Americas' great gallery of faces: stirrup-spout bottles whose chambers are modeled and painted portraits — old men with furrowed cheeks, warriors with face paint, scarred veterans and composed dignitaries — some individualized enough to suggest specific named lords. Built from coils and molds then reworked by hand, painted in cream-and-red slips, they likely portrayed elite individuals for court and burial; royal tombs like the Lord of Sipán confirm the portrait world's real-world cast. Rafael Larco Hoyle amassed thousands of 'huacos retrato,' now the core of the Larco Museum in Lima; the finer examples rival any ancient portrait tradition in psychological presence. The look: calm burnished faces under loop-and-spout handles, red-on-cream reserve painting.",
