    sum: "Mantles embroidered with flying shamans and severed heads — up to 190 yarn colors in desert burial bundles.",
    desc: "In the Paracas Necropolis's mummy bundles, Julio C. Tello's 1927 excavation found textiles of impossible richness: wool-on-cotton mantles whose borders swarm with embroidered 'Oculate Beings' — masked flying shamans, felines and condors clutching severed heads, each figure a variant on the last. Spinners and embroiderers, almost certainly women specialists, worked dyed camelid yarn in a chromatic range often tallied near 190 shades, from shrimp pink to deep indigo, using stem-stitch at near-microscopic density. The garments layered over elite dead across generations before final burial, making cemeteries archives of color. Conserved in Lima (MNAAHP) and Gothenburg — whose collection's 2014 repatriation controversy made headlines — Paracas embroidery anchors Andean textile history. The look: polychrome flying figures, border-to-border, glowing against desert dust.",
