    sum: "The world's largest mud-brick city — kilometre walls carved with fishnets, birds and otter processions.",
    desc: "Chan Chan, capital of Chimor and the largest adobe city on earth (some 20 km²), is architecture-as-frieze: ten royal ciudadelas enclosed by nine-meter walls whose interior corridors carry continuous low-relief carving — fishnet lattice dense with fish, bird processions, otters and squirrels, wave forms and geometric 'audiencia' panels, some once painted in mineral pigments over earth plaster. Mud brick and puddled adobe made a soft, soaring, monochrome architecture where shadow carves the ornament; storerooms, sunken plazas and burial platforms organized each walled royal compound, built by one king and sealed at his death. Inca conquest (c. 1470) and El Niño rains softened the city; UNESCO listing (1986) flags its fragility, and conservation shelters now roof the finest friezes. The look: golden-brown clay light, lace-precise relief, endless corridor perspective.",
