    sum: "Coin-silver headbands, pins and pectorals worn in shining abundance — and the machi's quartered cosmos drum.",
    desc: "After 18th-century peace parlamentos flooded Araucanía with silver coins, Mapuche smiths (plateros) melted and re-forged them into a dazzling female regalia: tupu shawl pins, trapelacucha pectorals, trariwe headbands, chaway earrings and silver-studded belts, chased and engraved in symmetric plant-and-star repoussé, worn layered at ngillatun ceremonies so a woman glittered like lineage itself. Alongside, the machi (healer) paints the kultrun drum's head with the four quarters of the Mapuche cosmos (meli witran mapu) and its stepped crosses, drumming it to travel and heal; the drum is both instrument and cosmological chart. Missionization and Chilean assimilation nearly stilled both arts; the late 20th-century Mapuche renaissance revived platería workshops and machi practice. The look: coin-bright silver on indigo cloth, painted drum thunder.",
