    sum: "Street-corner congas and voices — yambú, guaguancó and columbia turning courtyards into ritual theater.",
    desc: "Rumba grew in the solares and waterfront patios of Havana and Matanzas, where freed and enslaved-descended Cubans built ensemble art from conga drums, wooden cajón boxes, claves and call-and-response chorus. Its three faces: yambú, the slow 'dance of the old people'; guaguancó, the flirtatious pursuit sealed by the vacunao gesture; and columbia, the virtuoso solo male challenge balancing on genius footwork and drum dialogue. Long policed and racialized as low culture, rumba rose to national emblem through ensembles like Los Muñequitos de Matanzas and is UNESCO-listed (2016) as heritage of musicianship, dance and improvised verse. Open-air ruedas still erupt on Sunday afternoons in working-class barrios and at Callejón de Hamel; the visual world is sunlit street, bright dresses, drums on crates. The look: sweat-shine courtyard theater, drum circles, flying skirts.",
