    desc: "The vejigante — a horned, many-eyed comic devil with bat-wing capes and a cow-bladder noisemaker (the vejiga) — erupts through Puerto Rico's Fiestas de Santiago Apóstol in Loíza and the February Carnival of Ponce. Two craft schools define the look: Ponce's papier-mâché masks, exaggerated into dozens of horns and 'eyes' in candy colors, and Loíza's carved coconut-shell masks (from the Afro-Puerto Rican lineages of that town) with sharp teeth and domed crowns, both painted in high-saturation point-and-swirl patterns. African, Iberian and Taíno threads braid in the figure's genealogy; master mask-making families pass the trade down, and the mask now doubles as national art emblem in galleries and NMAI collections. The look: grinning horns, confetti paint, wings mid-lunge.",
