    sum: "Oricha-colored consecrated beads and Añá-filled batá drums — the orishas' colors and voices made wearable.",
    desc: "In Lucumí (Santería/Regla de Ocha) practice, eleke — necklaces of glass and stone beads — are consecrated by padrinos in oricha colors: Elegguá's red-and-black, Obatalá's all white, Yemayá's translucent blue, Changó's red-and-white — worn as protection and covenant, each collar a material oath. Beside them stand the batá drums (iyá, itótele, okónkolo), two-headed drums 'born' through the Añá consecration that installs the oricha of the drum itself; bembé toques call orichas with praise songs and 'talking' drum patterns from Yoruba Nigeria. Cowrie-shell diloggún divination boards, sopera lidded vessels and white sanctuary cloths complete a domestic-temple aesthetic of gleaming color under white light. Post-1959 exile carried the tradition to New York and Miami temple rooms; museums now collect both as diaspora sacred art. The look: bead-bright color codes, thunder of consecrated drums.",
